WEBVTT

00:00:00.105 --> 00:00:02.487
Your reasoning treated the conflict test

00:00:02.947 --> 00:00:05.316
as the definition of serializability.

00:00:05.737 --> 00:00:07.276
Let's pull the two ideas apart.

00:00:08.135 --> 00:00:09.977
View-serializability asks:

00:00:10.412 --> 00:00:13.780
same reads and same final writes as some

00:00:13.793 --> 00:00:14.635
serial schedule?

00:00:15.043 --> 00:00:17.543
Conflict-serializability is stricter:

00:00:18.004 --> 00:00:20.622
the same order on every conflicting pair.

00:00:21.043 --> 00:00:24.648
Every CSR schedule is VSR; the converse is

00:00:24.648 --> 00:00:26.109
not necessarily true.

00:00:26.941 --> 00:00:27.823
Look at this schedule.

00:00:28.244 --> 00:00:31.389
T2 and T1 write x without reading it

00:00:31.389 --> 00:00:31.928
first:

00:00:32.218 --> 00:00:33.362
these are blind writes.

00:00:33.783 --> 00:00:35.691
Their order creates conflicts in both

00:00:35.704 --> 00:00:38.547
directions, yet nobody ever reads what

00:00:38.547 --> 00:00:42.165
they wrote, because T3 overwrites x at the

00:00:42.165 --> 00:00:42.639
end.

00:00:43.441 --> 00:00:47.651
Against the serial order T1, T2, T3:

00:00:48.165 --> 00:00:52.151
T1 reads the initial x and T3 writes last,

00:00:52.375 --> 00:00:52.967
in both.

00:00:53.401 --> 00:00:55.257
So what does a conflict cycle really tell

00:00:55.257 --> 00:00:55.520
you?

00:00:55.941 --> 00:00:57.401
Try the next question yourself.
